<Layout title="About and sources — Folklore"><article class="prose-page"><h1>A name gives you<br/>somewhere to start.</h1><p class="lede">Folklore is a field guide for people who know what they mean before they know what to call it.</p><h2>From description to implementation</h2><p>Describe an element or style, inspect the example, and take a precise brief into your coding agent. The website and offline Codex package are generated from the same catalog.</p><h2>How entries are researched</h2><p>Element terminology and behavior are checked against platform documentation, web specifications, and accessibility guidance. API names stay attached to their frameworks. A generic pattern such as a drawer can have several valid implementations; the catalog describes the intended behavior rather than inventing a universal API.</p><p>Primary references include <a href="https://www.w3.org/WAI/ARIA/apg/patterns/">WAI-ARIA Authoring Practices</a>, <a href="https://developer.mozilla.org/">MDN’s web platform documentation</a>, and <a href="https://developer.apple.com/documentation/">Apple Developer Documentation</a>. Each entry links to the sources relevant to it.</p><h2>Styles need context</h2><p>Some style names describe historical movements. Others are industry shorthand, retrospective classifications, or contested labels. Folklore labels those differences and supplies visible signals plus sources or shipping specimens. A source specimen demonstrates a look; it does not establish a formal standard.</p><h2>The demonstrations</h2><p>Every specimen is authored for Folklore. Web examples demonstrate the interaction described on the page. macOS examples are browser simulations and cannot reproduce native window management or system materials. The small product names and content inside demonstrations are illustrative.</p><h2>Independent, with a reference</h2><p><a href="https://namethatui.com/">Name That UI</a> inspired the discovery, anatomy, and agent-prompt workflow. Folklore has its own interface, content, specimens, and offline packaging. It is not affiliated with Name That UI, Apple, or OpenAI.</p><p>Created by Parth Pawar. Corrections and contributions are welcome through <a href="https://github.com/Parthawe/folklore-ui/issues">GitHub issues</a>.</p></article></Layout>
